{"id": "DfHkuG8Mazn", "title": "Video by feartalkies", "description": "A woman fed a starving man sweet rice. His last words: \"You killed me.\" Her husband threw her out… yet she was innocent. 🍚\n\nLast tale's answer: Vikram said the man wearing her husband's head was her husband, because the head is the chief of the limbs, and who we are depends on it.\n\nVikram & Betaal: The Poisoned Rice.\n\nThe parts we couldn't fit in the reel:\n\nThe man was Harisvamin, from Varanasi, and his wife was Lavanyavati. The sky-prince who carried her off was Madanavega, a Vidyadhara, one of the flying sky-beings. When Harisvamin woke, he searched the roof, the turrets and the garden, thinking she might be hiding to tease him. The moon that had comforted him now burned him like hot coals.\n\nHis friends told him: \"She is not dead, so why do you kill yourself? Stay alive and you will find her.\" So he gave away everything he owned and set out for every holy bathing place, hoping to wash away his sins and find her.\n\nThe woman who fed him was the wife of a man named Padmanabha, in a village where a feast was going on. As the poison took hold, Harisvamin said: \"When fate turns against a man, even sweet rice becomes poison.\"\n\nThe woman went to a holy bathing place to do penance. The judges argued over who was guilty, the snake, the bird or the couple who gave the rice, and they never decided. (The old book calls the bird a kite.)\n\n(From Somadeva's Ocean of Story, the Vetala tales.)\n\nSo, who killed him? The kind woman who fed him… or the bird that dropped the poison? 👇\n\n#vikrambetaal #indianhorror #ghoststories #indianfolklore #halloween", "channel": "feartalkies", "uploader_id": "58803293522", "uploader": "Fear Talkies | Indian Horror", "timestamp": 1793363087, "like_count": 3799, "comment_count": 24, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/148.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,applicati